brief_description_of_drawing:
[0017] The systems and improved algorithms for carrying out the computer operations of the present invention for accessing and transferring data between a mass storage system and a host computer are graphically shown in the following drawings:
[0018] FIG. 1 depicts a pool of seventeen buffers, seven (7) of which contain data and ten (10) of which are empty. If writing, the write operations for the seven buffers con-taining issued;
[0019] FIG 2 depicts the parameter list for a composite system call containing multiple potentially independent requests (FIG. 2) for transfers between storage device(s) and a host computer;
[0020] FIG. 3 depicts a classic I/O Request Packet (IRP) for a conventional single request per "system call" con-structed from the parameters illustrated in FIG. 2;
[0021] FIG. 4 depicts the parameters for an I/O request to transfer a single buffer, specifying the function; completion/ status information; and the variable parameters (P1-6), which specify the buffer address, transfer address, and block number of the first subject storage block;
[0022] FIG. 5 depicts another improvement of the present invention, a composite data structure, referred to as a "multipacket", containing an administrative header and mul-tiple I/O Request Packets (FIG. 3), thus reducing the allo-cation/deallocation operations and contention for the asso-ciated memory allocation/deallocation locks, a significant performance bottleneck;
[0023] FIG. 6 depicts three basic examples of intercon-nection networks compatible with the methodology of the present invention; (A) depicts a simple network including a single host and a single terminal of that are directly con-nected; (B) depicts a network including a single host CPU and a plurality of terminal nodes connected to the host by a bus; and (C) depicts a switching network including a plu-rality of host CPUs each connected to a plurality of terminal nodes via a switching network;
[0024] FIG. 7 depicts an example of a suitable dialogue supporting deferred mapping of Buffer IDs in accordance with the methodology of the present invention; Incoming request ID 137(leftmost column) generates a request to map the associated buffer at time 12000, the mapping is released implicitly by the End ID 137 at time 20000;
[0025] FIG. 8 depicts the methodology of the present invention where requests arriving from a Requestor(s) are divided into work elements, where the block(s) referenced by each work element are contained within a natural group-ing (e.g., cylinder, in the case of moving-arm rotating storage) and queued separately for processing;
[0026] FIG. 9 depicts the transfer mapping of blocks within a track/cylinder to transfer addresses in I/O address space, with provision (2 entries) for skipping blocks, that is conceptually circular, so that the starting point is irrelevant in accordance with the methodology of the present inven-tion;
[0027] FIGS. 10-17 depict a series of flowcharts for a series of related or independent processes for carrying out the present invention;
[0028] FIGS. 10-12 depict the algorithms for the alloca-tion and deallocation of a multipacket and the individual request packets within a multipacket;
[0029] FIG. 13 depicts the flowchart for I/O request defer-ral in accordance with the present invention; enabling the batching of multiple buffers into a composite I/O system request (FIG. 4);
[0030] FIG. 14 depicts an algorithm depicting a method for achieving a virtual request expansion. It is particularly useful when used in conjunction with a multipacket (FIG. 5) and/or composite I/O requests (FIG. 4) and the related procedures illustrated in FIGS. 10-13;
[0031] FIG. 15 depicts the flowchart for a requested arrival at the terminal device and again is most effective when used in combination with FIGS. 10-14;
[0032] FIGS. 16 and 17 provide and describe the algo-rithms for creating a transfer map from the queue and then processing the transfer map, which again is especially effec-tive. As before, this combination of methods in these two flowcharts can be used by itself in improving the systems as presently used, but again results in a great improvement when used in combination with all of the preceding flow-charts.

claims:
1. A computing system comprising:
a CPU;
random access primary memory (RAM) accessible to the CPU;
a program executing on the CPU;
a buffer consisting of consecutive random access primary memory locations directly accessible via CPU instruc-tions;
a secondary storage device comprising one or more mass storage devices;
an I/O Interface providing a transfer mechanism between the random access primary memory and the secondary storage device;
a mass storage transfer request includes direction (to/from RAM), a starting address in primary memory, a starting mass storage block number, and a block count;
a controller configured to accept mass storage transfer requests from the I/O interface and control the second-ary storage device; and
an operating system configured to initiate data transfers between buffers located in random access primary memory to/from the secondary storage device;
one or more device drivers that translate program-issued operating system I/O requests into controller requests transferred via the I/O interface to the controller
wherein:
a program invokes an operating system service to enqueue a program request to initiate mass storage transfer requests between program buffers and the secondary mass storage device;
one or more mass storage devices comprise a sequential mass storage device;
at least one of the device drivers delays communication of the I/O transfer request until a specified backlog threshold measured in records and/or bytes has been reached;
the controller receives backlog data bytes and record count from the device driver.

3. A computing system comprising:
a CPU;
random access primary memory (RAM) accessible to the CPU;
a program executing on the CPU;
a buffer consisting of consecutive random access primary memory locations directly accessible via CPU instruc-tions;
a secondary storage device comprising one or more mass storage devices;
an I/O Interface providing a transfer mechanism between the random access primary memory and the secondary storage device;
a mass storage transfer request includes direction (to/from RAM), a starting address in primary memory, a starting mass storage block number, and a block count;
a controller configured to accept mass storage transfer requests from the I/O interface and control the second-ary storage device; and
an operating system configured to initiate data transfers between buffers located in random access primary memory to/from and the secondary storage device;
the one or more mass storage devices comprise a random access mass storage device wherein:
a program invokes an operating system service to enqueue a program request to initiate mass storage transfer requests between program buffers and the secondary mass storage device,
each request for consecutive blocks within a file is expanded into one or more sub-requests for consecu-tive blocks within a volume with each sub-request containing the corresponding starting buffer address, starting mass storage block number, and length, before any sub-requests are enqueued for processing.

6. A computing system comprising:
a CPU;
random access primary memory (RAM) accessible to the CPU;
a program executing on the CPU;
a buffer consisting of consecutive random access primary memory locations directly accessible via CPU instruc-tions;
a secondary storage device comprising one or more mass storage devices; and
an operating system configured to initiate data transfers between buffers located in random access primary memory to/from the secondary storage device wherein:
a program invokes an operating system service to enqueue multiple independent program I/O sub-requests, with each independent sub request contains a sub-request specific completion address, status return location, and possibly other values.

9. A computing system comprising:
a CPU;
random access primary memory (RAM) accessible to the CPU;
a program executing on the CPU;
a buffer consisting of consecutive random access primary memory locations directly accessible via CPU instruc-tions;
a secondary storage device comprising one or more mass storage devices;
